<h1>kth order Fibonacci numbers</h1>
<p>
(definition)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
A sequence of numbers where each number is the sum of the <em>k</em> preceding numbers. The usual <a href="fibonacciNumber.html"><em>Fibonacci numbers</em></a> occur when <em>k=2</em>.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Formal Definition:</strong> <ul> <li> <em>F<sup>(k)</sup><sub>n</sub></em> = <em>F<sup>(k)</sup><sub>n-1</sub></em> + <em>F<sup>(k)</sup><sub>n-2</sub></em> + . . . + <em>F<sup>(k)</sup><sub>n-k</sub></em> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; for  n &ge; k. <li><em>F<sup>(k)</sup><sub>k-1</sub></em> = 1 <li><em>F<sup>(k)</sup><sub>n</sub></em> = 0 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; for &nbsp;  0 &le; n &le; k-2 </ul>
</p>
<p>
<strong>Also known as</strong> pth order Fibonacci numbers.
</p>

<p>
<strong>See also</strong>
<a href="optimpolymrg.html"><em>optimal polyphase merge</em></a>.
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>
<h2>More information</h2>
<p>
Apparently first studied by <strong>V. Schlegel</strong>, El Progreso Matem&aacute;tico, 4:173-174, 1894.
</p>

